<Base title="Replication and failover" description="Distinguish copied, durable and readable changes; choose acknowledgement and read-routing rules; and preserve one authoritative writer during failover.">
 <meta slot="head" name="robots" content="noindex" />
 <article class="visual-essay relational-design">
  <header class="essay-header"><p class="eyebrow">Databases and storage · Working draft</p><h1>Replication and failover</h1><div class="essay-meta"><span>Relational further reading</span><a href="/learn/databases/relational/scaling/">Scaling a relational database ↗</a></div></header>
  <p><strong>Replication</strong> maintains another copy of database state by carrying changes from one place to another. That copy can provide resources for reads or a starting point for recovery when the writer fails. Its usefulness depends on which changes have arrived, which are durable, and which a query can already see.</p>
  <p>Those are separate events. A server can receive a change into memory before saving it to storage. It can save the change log before applying the change to the tables used by readers. The application’s success reply may occur before or after particular events, depending on the acknowledgement policy. “There is a replica” does not answer which purchase survives a failure or which price the next read returns.</p>
  <p>We will follow PostgreSQL 18 with writer A and standby B. A blue mug initially costs €20 on both. An application changes it to €25 on A. This one change lets us trace read freshness, the success reply and the decision to make B the new writer. Other engines can implement different replication and election protocols; keep their guarantees tied to their own documented configuration.</p>
  <nav aria-label="In this article"><ol><li><a href="#copying">Copying a change</a></li><li><a href="#acknowledgements">What success confirms</a></li><li><a href="#reads">Routing a fresh read</a></li><li><a href="#promotion">Replacing the writer</a></li><li><a href="#regions">Failure domains and regions</a></li><li><a href="#operate">Operating the copies</a></li></ol></nav>

  <section id="copying"><h2>Copying a change</h2>
   <p>PostgreSQL’s physical streaming replication carries the writer’s <strong>write-ahead log</strong>, or WAL, to the standby. WAL records the changes needed for recovery. B saves received records and replays them to bring its own database forward. An initial consistent base copy and the subsequent log history belong together; a handful of table copies without the required history is not the same arrangement.</p>
   <p>For the mug update, picture three positions: A has committed the change; B has saved its log record; B has replayed it. They need not advance together. We will call the completed change position 2 and the previous state position 1. These are invented sequence numbers for the illustration. PostgreSQL identifies locations within WAL using log sequence numbers, or LSNs, and recovery history also matters when comparing positions.</p>
   <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="The price change at three copying stages"><table><thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>A’s price</th><th>B’s saved log through</th><th>B’s new ordinary read</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A commits; B has not received the change</td><td>€25</td><td>Position 1</td><td>€20</td></tr><tr><td>B saves position 2</td><td>€25</td><td>Position 2</td><td>€20 until replay</td></tr><tr><td>B replays position 2</td><td>€25</td><td>Position 2</td><td>€25</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
   <p>The second row is often the important surprise. B may possess the durable information required to recover the €25 price while its current queryable state still says €20. A new snapshot can see a committed change after its commit record has been replayed. A transaction already holding an older snapshot follows its isolation rules instead of automatically jumping to the latest state.</p>
   <p>The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=replica-freshness">replica-freshness lesson</a> represents these stages in ordinary SQL tables. You explicitly move the model from received to applied and predict its reads. It does not start separate servers, copy real WAL or test storage durability.</p>
   <p>Logical replication is another kind of copying. It communicates selected logical changes rather than reproducing the entire physical storage layout. That can help selective movement or integration, but changes the operating contract: PostgreSQL’s logical replication does not automatically carry all schema changes or sequence state. The word replication alone does not establish that a destination is ready to replace its source.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="acknowledgements"><h2>What success confirms</h2>
   <p>With asynchronous streaming, A does not wait for B before acknowledging the application’s commit. Under the normal local durability settings, A has saved what it needs locally. B may still be at position 1. If A and its storage are permanently lost at that moment, a surviving B cannot reconstruct position 2 from a record it never received. If another retained copy or archive has it, recovery may have more options; the guarantee comes from those surviving records.</p>
   <p>This makes normal commits independent of B’s reply, which can be useful when a distant or overloaded standby is temporarily unavailable. It also means the service must accept the possible loss of recently acknowledged changes under the stated failure. “Usually only a little behind” is an observation, not a loss bound.</p>
   <p>A synchronous policy waits for specified standby acknowledgements. In PostgreSQL, the meaning depends on <code>synchronous_commit</code> together with the configured synchronous standbys. With <code>synchronous_standby_names</code> empty, setting <code>synchronous_commit = on</code> does not by itself wait for a remote copy.</p>
   <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="PostgreSQL synchronous commit acknowledgement distinctions"><table><thead><tr><th>Policy with synchronous standbys configured</th><th>What the required standby confirms</th><th>What can remain unfinished</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>remote_write</code></td><td>The record reached the standby’s filesystem writes</td><td>Durable storage across an operating-system failure; replay</td></tr><tr><td><code>on</code></td><td>The record was flushed to durable storage</td><td>Replay into query-visible state</td></tr><tr><td><code>remote_apply</code></td><td>The record was durably saved and applied</td><td>Catch-up on other, non-confirming replicas; an older reader snapshot</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
   <p>For the mug, the second stage in our table can satisfy a synchronous durable-copy policy, but only the third satisfies the corresponding apply requirement. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/runtime-config-wal.html#GUC-SYNCHRONOUS-COMMIT">PostgreSQL’s commit settings</a> define these distinctions. Storage must still honour the writes it acknowledges, and the chosen failure must leave a confirming copy available.</p>
   <p>Waiting changes both latency and availability. If a required standby is slow, A’s success reply waits. If the required acknowledgements cannot be obtained, accepting more local work does not produce the promised synchronous success. Choosing a quorum of eligible standbys can allow some to be unavailable, but the count and placement must match the failure being covered. A local quorum whose members all disappear with one region does not protect against that region’s loss.</p>
   <p>A connection failure or cancellation while waiting is not proof that the transaction rolled back. The local commit and its reply can be separated by failure. The application must preserve the operation identity and resolve its outcome as described in <a href="/learn/databases/relational/conflicts-and-retries/">Handling conflicts and uncertain outcomes</a>. Changing to a weaker acknowledgement policy during an incident also changes which later successes are protected; that needs an explicit service decision.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="reads"><h2>Routing a fresh read</h2>
   <p>The customer changes the mug’s price and immediately opens the product page. If that read goes to B before replay, seeing €20 does not mean the update failed. It means the selected read source does not yet include it. Blindly retrying the update could repeat a larger operation that already succeeded.</p>
   <p>A simple policy sends reads that must follow a user’s write to the authoritative writer. Other requests, such as a catalogue view allowed to be slightly behind, can use replicas. Make the distinction in the application’s read contract; the SQL text alone does not say whether an old answer is acceptable.</p>
   <p>Another policy carries a progress requirement from the write. Before serving the next read, the router waits until the selected copy has applied at least the required committed position, then begins an appropriate new read snapshot. If the wait exceeds the request’s budget, it can use a suitable writer path, return a pending result or report that the freshness requirement cannot currently be met. It must not quietly return an older result as though the check succeeded.</p>
   <p>The progress token needs an engine-specific definition. A real PostgreSQL deployment must relate it to the completed transaction and the correct recovery lineage, not compare arbitrary integers from different writers. The browser model uses position 2 only to teach the requirement. A fixed sleep of 100 milliseconds after writing has no equivalent proof: replay can take longer, stop, or move to a different eligible copy.</p>
   <p>Synchronous durable-copy acknowledgement alone does not solve the read problem, because saving is not applying. Even an apply acknowledgement concerns the standby or standbys that actually confirmed it. A load balancer that then chooses a different lagging replica can still return the old price. Existing transaction snapshots must also be considered when a connection is reused.</p>
   <p>For checkout decisions, use the authoritative transaction path. A stale replica can help display a product, but a read there cannot reserve stock on A. The writer must still test and perform the change together. Read scaling should preserve the boundary where a business decision becomes authoritative.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="promotion"><h2>Replacing the writer</h2>
   <p><strong>Failover</strong> moves service to a replacement after a failure. Promotion makes a standby writable, but safe failover also needs a decision about who may write. Suppose the application loses contact with A. A might have crashed, or a network link between the observer and A might have failed while A continues serving other callers. Both begin with the same timeout.</p>
   <p>If B starts accepting independent writes while A can still do so, the two histories can diverge. A may reserve the last mug for one buyer while B reserves its copy for another. Each local transaction can satisfy its constraints. Later reconnecting the servers does not tell the business which completed purchase to erase.</p>
   <p>The old writer must therefore be unable to continue accepting authoritative writes before the replacement is exposed. This is often called <strong>fencing</strong>. Depending on the system, it may mean stopping or powering off the old node, revoking access to writable storage, or enforcing a writer lease or generation at the place every write is admitted. Updating a DNS name or changing a router’s cached address does not stop an old connection from writing.</p>
   <p>PostgreSQL can promote a standby, but its <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/warm-standby-failover.html">failover documentation</a> leaves failure detection and coordination to surrounding tools and stresses preventing the old primary from returning as a writer. A managed service may supply that machinery; an operator assembling servers must establish it. If the surviving system cannot establish exclusive authority safely, pausing writes is a possible required outcome of the promised consistency.</p>
   <ol><li><strong>Establish authority.</strong> Decide which participant is permitted to replace A and make the old write path unusable. Include already-open connections.</li><li><strong>Choose a recoverable candidate.</strong> Inspect the surviving log history and the acknowledgement policy. A node being reachable does not make it the most complete copy.</li><li><strong>Finish recovery and promote.</strong> Apply the history available to the candidate, then make it writable under the chosen authority.</li><li><strong>Move clients and resolve attempts.</strong> Refresh endpoints and pools; retry known rejections or recover uncertain operation identities.</li><li><strong>Rebuild protection.</strong> Rejoin the former writer only after reconciling its state with the chosen history, and restore enough healthy copies.</li></ol>
   <p>In our asynchronous stage-one failure, promoting B exposes the €20 history. In stage two, B has saved the €25 change and can replay it before serving as writer. A policy that waited for B’s durable acknowledgement can therefore protect that commit against A’s loss, provided B’s saved copy survives and the failover actually uses a sufficiently complete history. Promoting an unrelated lagging C would not inherit B’s guarantee.</p>
   <p>Returning A is a recovery task, not an automatic reversal of the endpoint change. Once B accepts new writes, old A lacks them. It may also have a divergent history. The platform must reconcile or rebuild A as an eligible copy before trusting it again. The same principle applies to <a href="/learn/databases/relational/partitioning-and-sharding/#moving-data">moving a shard</a>: a retained old copy is not necessarily a safe rollback target.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="regions"><h2>Failure domains and regions</h2>
   <p>A failure domain is a set of components that can become unavailable together. Different processes on one machine share its operating system and hardware. Different machines may share a storage service, power supply, network or availability zone. A region can also contain the control service, credentials or routing endpoint needed to complete failover elsewhere.</p>
   <p>For the shop, compare two contracts. A nearby synchronous copy may protect acknowledged purchases against a single writer-machine failure with a short normal network path. A distant asynchronous copy may help recover from loss of the whole region, but it can be missing the most recent successes. Those copies serve different failure contracts even if both are described as replicas.</p>
   <p>If acknowledged purchases must survive complete loss of the writer’s region, the relevant success condition must require a surviving copy outside it, or another mechanism with that guarantee. That remote path now affects the commit. During a network separation, the system may have to withhold successful writes rather than allow both sides to make independent promises. This is a choice about the service’s required outcomes, not an option to obtain every guarantee without a cost.</p>
   <p><strong>Recovery point objective</strong> describes how much data loss the service is prepared to tolerate. <strong>Recovery time objective</strong> describes how quickly it needs to restore service. A remote copy being current addresses only part of the second objective: detection, authority, replay, routing, connection recovery and sufficient surviving capacity all take work. Measure those steps in a drill instead of inferring a recovery time from ordinary replication lag.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="operate"><h2>Operating the copies</h2>
   <p>On a PostgreSQL writer, an operator with suitable monitoring privileges can inspect connected standbys. This query is for a real replicated server; it does not create replication in the sandbox:</p>
   <Sql code={progress} label="Inspect connected PostgreSQL standbys on the writer" />
   <p>The positions describe different progress: sent, written, flushed and replayed. Compare them with the write and read promises, and investigate where progress stalls. Byte distance between log positions is not a direct estimate of seconds to recover: incoming change volume and replay capacity vary. Observe trends, apply progress and end-to-end freshness using representative changes.</p>
   <p>A standby that falls behind needs access to the intervening log. Retaining that log can consume storage on the writer or in an archive. A disconnected copy must not be allowed to exhaust the writer’s disk unnoticed; limiting retention, rebuilding an excessively old copy and keeping a usable archive are operating choices. Monitor the retention mechanism actually configured.</p>
   <p>Test reads during replay as well as writes during failover. A long PostgreSQL standby query can conflict with cleanup or schema changes already committed on A. Delaying replay preserves the query’s older view but increases lag; cancelling the query requires application retry. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/hot-standby.html#HOT-STANDBY-CONFLICT">hot-standby conflict rules</a> explain the tradeoff.</p>
   <p>Finally, keep backups and a tested restore path. A valid but unwanted deletion can replicate quickly and accurately to every standby. Copies of the latest state help replace failed infrastructure; retained earlier recovery points help undo some mistakes. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/backup-and-restore/">Backup and restore</a> follows that different recovery question.</p>
  </section>
